15 million web servers on one VM - smoltcp, DPDK, AF_XDP · kapteko Skip to content kapteko Articles About me 15 million web servers on one VM - smoltcp, DPDK, AF_XDP Nov 18, 2026 Put a web server online and everyone starts trying to hack it. Why? A basic rule of economics. Short supply, big demand - there aren’t enough web servers for everyone. Let’s run a web server for every human on the planet, all 8 billion of them. I couldn’t get funding, so we’re bootstrapping. The goal is to run 15 million web servers on one VM with 10GB of RAM and 1 CPU core. If we succeed, I’ll find some RAM from old microwaves around the house and we’ll scale it up so everyone can have their own web server. Demand will drop to zero and cybersecurity will be solved once and for all. Web server Instead of vibe coding it all, let’s use an ancient technique called learning . First of all, what is a web server? There are a few essential syscalls. listen - syscall, asks kernel to listen for connections on a socket accept - syscall, accepts a connection on a socket recv - receives data from a socket send - sends data close - closes What is a syscall? A function that is executed by the OS kernel. Whatever fancy language or library you use for HTTP, it all comes down to those syscalls (well, yes, but actually no). Let’s use C to make it all explicit. We start listening, then read from the socket in a loop and always reply with OK . int main ( void ) 
{ 
// We use port 0 which makes OS return any available port 
int server = socket ( AF_INET , SOCK_STREAM , 0 ); 
if ( listen ( server , 128 ) < 0 ) 
return 1 ; 
const char reply [] = "HTTP/1.0 200 OK \r\n Content-Length: 2 \r\n\r\n OK" ; 
for ( int client ; ( client = accept ( server , NULL , NULL )) >= 0 ;) { 
if ( recv ( client , NULL , 4096 , MSG_TRUNC ) > 0 ) 
send ( client , reply , sizeof reply - 1 , MSG_NOSIGNAL ); 
close ( client ); 
} 
} It couldn’t be simpler: listen on a socket, accept a connection, read the data, send a reply, close the client connection, and repeat. It gives us around 65k RPS. Now that we’ve learned everything, let’s finish the article and call it a day: $ for i in $( seq 1 15_000_000 ); do echo $i ; ./server &; done 
... 
... 
28232 
program exited with error 1 Hm, why did listen fail? Ah, we’ve run out of ports! We’re using port=0 , so the kernel picks an available port from ip_local_port_range . $ sysctl net.ipv4.ip_local_port_range 
net.ipv4.ip_local_port_range = 32768 60999 Why are so many ports skipped? Historically, port 0 was created for outbound connections. When you call connect('example.com', 80) , Linux connects to the remote server (the sending part) and then allocates a random port on the host from ip_local_port_range to receive data (the receiving part). There are a few reasons the default range is small. Port 0 is special. You cannot listen on it (actually, you can - https://acid.vegas/blog/the-port-that-does-not-exist/ ). Ports 1-1024 require root. Ports 1024-32768 are skipped to keep dynamically allocated ports from conflicting with existing apps. You don’t want to call connect('example.com') and end up allocating port 3306 to receive incoming data. mysql cannot start because you have example.com open in your Firefox would make for a confusing UX. Ports 60999 - 65535 are excluded for historical reasons dating back to IP Masquerading. Nothing prevents us from overriding the range and using 1-65535 , but that’s still a bit short of 15 million. A UDP server and a TCP server can use the same port, so we can double that to 128k. We could run a proxy in front to forward requests to the right process (virtual hosting, yay), but these are all ancient techniques - in the AI era, whenever you have a software problem, you just throw money at the hardware. Let’s simply connect 15M/32k=450 new network cards to the server. The 65k limit comes from the TCP standard. A port is a u16 , so even if we hack the kernel with find '*.c' -exec sed -i 's/u16/u32/' \; to support bigger port numbers, normal clients still wouldn’t be able to connect. Each listening server is identified by a (protocol, IP, port) tuple. That explains how we can use the same port for UDP and TCP. It also means we can use a bunch of different IPs for our web servers. If we connect 450 network cards to the server, we’ll get 450 unique IP addresses and be able to listen on 15 million unique addresses! UPD. I’ve heard from readers that the network card is the ONLY hardware component that didn’t get 100 times more expensive in the last year. Can’t we avoid ruining that too and solve this in software? Let’s use a beautiful technology called AnyIP , which lets us listen on a whole subnet. Many VPS and dedicated server providers routinely give you a whole TCPv6 subnet. We can simply run sudo ip -6 route add local 2001:db8:1234:5678::/64 dev lo to get 18 quintillion addresses (plus or minus one) to listen on. You can do the same for IPv4 - acquiring a subnet with 15M addresses would cost around $250 million, so adjust the command for your environment. We’ll change our server to pick the nth IP from our range: // Read IP index from CLI, shift base IP by it 
int i = read_cli_param ( argv [ 1 ]); 
const uint32_t base_ip = 0x 0a400001 ; // ex. 10.64.0.1 
addr.sin_addr.s_addr = htonl ( first_ip + i ); // shift, ex. 10.64.0.2 And now we can finally finish the article. $ for i in $( seq 1 15_000_000 ); do echo $i ; ./server $i &; done 
... 
... 
Out of memory Hm, one server consumes 1.5MB of RAM, so running that many would take… 20TB of RAM, which is a few bytes more than our 10GB budget. Runtime By pure coincidence (what are the odds?), I explained in Running 10 million Codex CLIs on one server - LFI processes why running millions of processes creates too much overhead. The answer is to run them all in one process inside a custom runtime, much as V8 can run millions of guests in a single process. In Userland Linux scheduler in Rust , I showed how to build a specialised userland scheduler and switch between tasks with the absolute minimum amount of RAM per task. TLDR: We’re using LFI for sandboxing. It gives us memory isolation and replaces all syscalls with calls to our host handler, which lets us implement I/O however we like. To schedule multiple guests, we load them all into our main process’s memory and make the CPU constantly switch between guests - we become our own micro OS. Since we need to save every byte and CPU cycle we can, we’re doomed to build another specialised runtime. Let’s get started. How much is 10GB divided by 15M, exactly? Ten Great Britains divided by 15 million gives around half of kilo britain. Not much. If we run all our guests in our runtime, our custom scheduler needs to switch between them. What’s the absolute minimum state we need to preserve for each guest? We can ignore server initialisation and assume switches only happen within the main loop. Let’s take another look at the loop. for ( int client ; ( client = accept ( server , NULL , NULL )) >= 0 ;) { 
if ( recv ( client , NULL , 4096 , MSG_TRUNC ) > 0 ) 
send ( client , reply , sizeof reply - 1 , MSG_NOSIGNAL ); 
close ( client ); 
} This code has only two state variables, server and client . Both are simple file descriptors. If our scheduler had to suspend a guest at any syscall point in this loop, we’d need to save the following state: struct Context { 
pc : u64 , // Instruction address to return to 
fd_server : i32 , // server file descriptor 
fd_client : i32 , // client file descriptor 
} We only need 16 bytes per guest, so to run 15M guests in our runtime and be able to suspend them and switch between them, we would need 220MB of RAM. We’re playing it safe here - we don’t need the full u64 or i32 , and with bit packing, we could probably get down to 8 bytes per guest. What else do we need? $ codex "How much TCP connection takes?" 
Idle connection takes zero, while active connection consumes around 5kb Here’s what we get if we assume that only 1M of connections are active at any given moment. 14M idle connections - 0 bytes Scheduling state for 15M guests - 220MB 1M active connections at 5kb each = 5GB Only half of our RAM budget, yay! I don’t often exceed my own expectations. Let’s validate it: int main ( void ) { 
// Run 14M of idle connections - just listen and do nothing 
// SHOULD CONSUMER ZERO MEMORY! 
for ( int i = 0 ; i < 14_000_000 ; i ++ ) { 
int server = socket ( AF_INET , SOCK_STREAM , 0 ); 
listen ( server , 1 ) 
} 
} $ ./run_15m_idle_servers 
Out Of Memory 
$ dmesg | tail -n 1 
'SHOULD CONSUMER ZERO MEMORY' - what are you, stupid? Looks like the kernel still has some leftover comments from Linus. I’m not stupid, but I have to admit I was below average here. While an LLM is a significant boost for learning, it’s too easy to accept its answers without thinking critically and feel ashamed later on. But hey, embarrassment is a cost of entry - let’s dive into networking in the kernel. Networking in the kernel Remember when I said that our simple server uses only 5 syscalls? I missed one that was right in front of me. Let me highlight it: int main ( void ) 
{ 
for ( int i = 0 ; i < 10_000_000 ; ++ i ) { 
socket ( AF_INET , SOCK_STREAM , 0 ); 
} 
} That code will OOM in a 10GB VM. The socket syscall allocates around 3300 bytes, whether the socket is active, listening, or doing nothing. Linux was clearly written at a time when RAM was big and cheap, sigh. Let’s ask AI where all the memory goes: Struct sizeof Description struct tcp_sock 2240B TCP state, queues, timers, sequence numbers, congestion control, retransmit and delivery bookkeeping struct socket_alloc 768B A 128-byte struct socket with an embedded wait queue, plus a 608-byte sockfs inode and padding struct file 184B Open-file operations, flags, credentials, reference count, and the pointer back to the socket struct dentry 192B The sockfs pseudo-name, inode pointer, and VFS lookup and lifetime bookkeeping Total 3384B Potato-potato, TLDR, the kernel uses that memory for many things. I was hoping to see some buffer: [u8; 3300] , but it doesn’t look like we can optimise much without rewriting half the kernel. It’s Sunday and I have few tokens left, so we’ll have to think instead - how can we bypass the kernel? What are our options? Engineering wisdom tells us that if we can’t solve a task, we’re working at the wrong level of abstraction. Hm… level, level, layer. Wait a minute! We started with HTTP and went level down to TCP/IP. That reminds me of something! Mom! Mom! Where are all my university books? I NEED THEM NOW! The OSI model is… um… something I studied at university. Yeah, that’s a good definition. We started with our app and HTTP at the application layer, then went down to TCP/IP at the transport layer. There are three more layers below that. Can we go lower to get full control and solve our RAM problem? Oh yes, that’s exactly what operating at the network and data link layers lets us do. What about going to the lowest? Clear. Each layer wraps the data from the layer above it. HTTP Application · L7 TCP Transport · L4 IP Network · L3 Ethernet Data link · L2 Physical L1 GET / Payload Payload Payload ⚡ 💩 Src port 50123 Dst port 80 Seq 1001 ACK 7001 Flags ACK Src IP 192.0. 2.10 Dst IP 198.51. 100.20 TTL 64 Protocol 6 (TCP) Dst MAC 02:00:00: 00:00:fe Src MAC 02:00:00: 00:00:01 EtherType 0x0800 (IPv4) FCS CRC TCP/IP is stateful, but everything below is stateless. None of the lower layers know or care about the payload, so nothing prevents us from sending new IP packets or even Ethernet frames with our own handcrafted binary data. We just need to reimplement the TCP and IP stack ourselves. Are there any examples? Embedded folks have solved this millions of times. The most extreme case is Adam Dunkels’s Miniweb - it combines a custom TCP/IP implementation and an HTTP server that together need only 28 bytes (B-Y-T-E-S) to work. There are plenty of less extreme, more mature implementations, though: gVisor essentially reimplements the whole OS, including the network stack Seastar is a whole runtime with its own network stack from the amazing team behind ScyllaDB lwIP is a popular C implementation of TCP/IP F-Stack uses FreeBSD’s TCP/IP stack Go, C++, and C are nice languages (well, kinda), but they’re nowhere near as nice as our beloved Rust. So we’ll take smoltcp , which is purely amazing: >smoltcp is a standalone, event-driven TCP/IP stack that is designed for 
>bare-metal, real-time systems. smoltcp does not need heap allocation at all So, cargo add smoltcp and call it a day? Not so fast. smoltcp is essentially a smart parser and can’t do I/O. How does it communicate with your network card? There are 3 ways: TUN is a virtual network interface that the kernel provides. It operates at the IP layer TAP is another kernel feature that operates one layer down, at the Ethernet layer DPDK lets you communicate directly with the network card using Ethernet frames, completely bypassing the kernel smoltcp is kind enough to provide the first two options out of the box, and they’re easy to use. fn main () -> std :: io :: Result < () > { 
let ( mut device , config ) = match std :: env :: args () . nth ( 1 ) . as_deref () { 
Some ( "tun" ) => ( 
TunTapInterface :: new ( "smoltun" , Medium :: Ip ) ? , 
Config :: new ( HardwareAddress :: Ip ), 
), 
Some ( "tap" ) => ( 
TunTapInterface :: new ( "smoltap" , Medium :: Ethernet ) ? , 
Config :: new ( EthernetAddress ([ 0x02 , 0 , 0 , 0 , 0 , 2 ]) . into ()), 
), 
_ => panic! ( "pass tun or tap" ), 
}; 
let mut iface = Interface :: new ( config , & mut device , Instant :: now ()); 
iface . update_ip_addrs ( | addrs | { 
addrs . push ( IpCidr :: new ( IpAddress :: v4 ( 192 , 0 , 2 , 2 ), 24 )) . unwrap (); 
}); 
let mut sockets = SocketSet :: new ( Vec :: new ()); 

// Main network loop 
loop { 
let now = Instant :: now (); 
iface . poll ( now , & mut device , & mut sockets ); 
wait ( device . as_raw_fd (), iface . poll_delay ( now , & sockets )) ? ; 
} 
} But what about the third option, DPDK ? When you care about latency A LOT, every syscall is a bloody wound. The syscall itself might take 500ns, and copying data from the kernel to userspace might take another 1000ns, depending on the buffer size. That sounds tiny, but for perspective: it’s common for high-frequency trading firms to saturate 400Gbs network interfaces. That means processing up to 600 million packets per second, or roughly 1.7ns per packet. DPDK lets your program communicate directly with the hardware network card using Ethernet frames, bypassing the entire kernel network stack. You read from and write to a memory-mapped buffer without syscalls: when you want to send data - you simply write it to the buffer and you’re done; when you want to read what you’ve received - it’s already there. There’s another, less obvious advantage - what happens when a new packet arrives at your network device? An interrupt fires, the CPU core stops executing your app, switches to the kernel, processes the interrupt, and then goes back to your app. Now imagine A LOT of incoming packets, so many that your app gets interrupted all the time. It has a beautiful name, livelock , and I guess debugging it in production is its own level of hell. DPDK lets you avoid all that, but there’s a cost. DPDK works by running a busy loop that takes up an entire CPU core. It constantly moves data between memory and the network device in both directions, kinda like that: loop { 
send_from_app_memory_to_network (); 
receive_from_network_to_app_memory (); 
} There’s another, less obvious nuance. DPDK requires exclusive access to the network device because it uses a driver that operates at the hardware level - two drivers can’t work with the same device at the same time. Once you enable the DPDK driver for your network interface… it disappears from the OS. Yep, nothing in ifconfig , if you enable it over SSH, you’ll lose SSH access too. I can imagine a lot of funny stories coming out of that. Actually, there’s a fourth option now - Linux now has an AF_XDP subsystem that does something similar. It lets you bypass the kernel network stack and uses the same idea of memory-mapped buffers to get zero-copy I/O in your app. The kernel still owns the device and interrupts still happen as usual. When a new packet arrives, the kernel runs a simple eBPF filter that decides whether to send it directly to the userspace buffer, bypassing the network stack, or process it normally. That means SSH still works. AF_XDP supports an alternative mode, SO_PREFER_BUSY_POLL , which is even closer to DPDK : you get a busy loop with no interrupts at the cost of a CPU core. DPDK still beats it because it bypasses the kernel completely, but AF_XDP is very close (and simply easier to manage). To support DPDK and AF_XDP in smoltcp , we only need to implement the Device trait with 3 methods if we want full control. Alternatively, we can use the existing crates rust-dpdk-net for DPDK and xsk-rs for AF_XDP to help us. Now the final flow is: The runtime loads 15M web server guests into its memory The runtime scheduler starts executing guests one by one Guest syscalls are intercepted by the runtime and replaced by the smoltcp network stack and its adapters All 15M guests start listening, each on its own address The scheduler polls for incoming data from smoltcp adapters, wakes up a guest, makes it process the data, and switches to the next And now we can finally finish the article. The kernel network stack uses up all the RAM at around 2.5M web servers, and RPS drops as we run more servers. With smoltcp , RAM usage grows slower - 620 bytes per idle server and around a kilobyte for an active one, we got to 16M of servers. DPDK is clearly the beast in this benchmark, with AF_XDP just behind it: Why is this so exciting? It means that by moving the network stack to userland, you can get full control on RAM usage and improve performance. And with custom runtime, you can simply run guests faster. Simply is a bit of a stretch - building a proof of concept is easy. Building a compatible Linux runtime and reimplementing all the syscalls with 100% POSIX compatibility take a bit more thought. And another article. References https://github.com/artemyarulin/AI10M/tree/main/poc/ten-million-webservers source https://medium.com/beyond-localhost/how-we-handle-15M-websocket-connections-on-a-single-server-8917c3952f59 great overview of DPDK https://doc.dpdk.org/guides/index.html DPDK main documentation site https://docs.kernel.org/networking/af_xdp.html AF_XDP overview ← All articles Articles · About me

